        {
          "text": "I’d replace “irrelevant” with “not decisive on its own.” That preserves your central intuition without making every continuous physical transformation count as survival. A revised position might be:\n\nA ship can remain the same individual despite replacement of all its original material. Its identity depends primarily on the causal history through which it is maintained as an organised vessel, rather than on possession of particular planks. Nevertheless, material and structural changes matter when they turn maintenance or repair into destruction and replacement.\n\nThe key distinction is between retaining original matter and preserving an object through changes in matter. Repair can do the latter without doing the former. Burning the ship preserves a causal connection to its remains but not, ordinarily, the ship. And retaining every plank in a pile doesn’t by itself preserve an assembled vessel.\n\nThis also leaves room for temporary loss of function: a ship in dry dock needn’t stop existing because it cannot currently sail. So neither original material nor uninterrupted seaworthiness is the sole test of numerical identity.\n\nI’d present this as a better starting principle, not a complete solution. We still need to explain the boundary between repair and destruction, especially in dismantling and reconstruction cases. But the useful shift is clear: material replacement is compatible with identity; material change is not automatically irrelevant to it.",
          "signals": {
